{"the_rehearsal_ratio_determining_the_percentage_of_sampled_training_instances": "The rehearsal ratio is a parameter that determines the proportion of training instances that are sampled during the rehearsal phase of training a language model. This ratio is crucial for balancing the model's exposure to new and old data during incremental learning."},
{"d_t__is_used_to__fine_tune_the_llm_\u03b8_t_1": "\"is used to\" indicates the purpose or function of _D_ [(] _[t]_ [)]. In this context, it signifies that the dataset _D_ [(] _[t]_ [)] serves as a means to adjust and improve the language model _\u03b8_ [(] _[t][\u2212]_ [1)]."},
{"fine_tune_the_llm_\u03b8_t_1": "The LLM _\u03b8_ [(] _[t][\u2212]_ [1)] represents the language model at time _t-1_, before it is fine-tuned using the dataset _D_ [(] _[t]_ [)]. Fine-tuning this model involves adjusting its parameters to better fit the new data, resulting in an updated version _\u03b8_ [(] _[t]_ [)]."},
{"llm_\u03b8_t_1": "The subject, LLM _\u03b8_ [(] _[t][\u2212]_ [1)], refers to a specific version of a large language model (LLM) that has been trained up to a certain point in time, denoted as _[t][\u2212]_ [1). This model represents the state of the LLM before undergoing further fine-tuning."},
{"llm_\u03b8_t_1__is_fine_tuned_to_obtain__the_updated_llm_\u03b8_t": "The predicate, \"is fine-tuned to obtain,\" describes the process of adjusting and optimizing the parameters of the LLM _\u03b8_ [(] _[t][\u2212]_ [1)] using a specific dataset _D_ [(] _[t]_ [)]. This fine-tuning aims to enhance the model's performance and adapt it to new data or tasks."},
{"the_updated_llm_\u03b8_t": "The object, the updated LLM _\u03b8_ [(] _[t]_ [)], represents the version of the large language model after it has undergone the fine-tuning process. This updated model incorporates the improvements and adaptations made during the fine-tuning, resulting in enhanced performance and capabilities compared to the previous version."},
{"refined_synthetic_instances": "Refined synthetic instances refer to a set of carefully curated and processed examples that have been synthesized for a specific purpose, likely within a technical or scientific context.|Here is another description: Refined synthetic instances refer to a curated set of artificial data points that have been processed and improved to better represent real-world scenarios. These instances are generated through various algorithms and techniques to enhance their quality and diversity."},
{"refined_synthetic_instances__are__\ud835\udc65_\u0d24\ud835\udc66": "The predicate \"are\" in this context signifies the state of being or existence of the refined synthetic instances, indicating that they currently exist in the specified form or set."},
{"\ud835\udc65_\u0d24\ud835\udc66": "The object {(\u0ddc\ud835\udc65, \u0d24\ud835\udc66)} represents a set of paired elements, where each pair consists of two components denoted by \u0ddc\ud835\udc65 and \u0d24\ud835\udc66. These could be variables, coordinates, or other types of data points that are part of the refined synthetic instances."},
{"the_square_root_of_16": "The square root of 16 refers to the number which, when multiplied by itself, results in 16. It is a fundamental concept in mathematics, specifically within the realm of arithmetic and algebra."},
{"the_square_root_of_16__is__4": "The predicate \"is\" in this context signifies an equation or equality, indicating that the subject (the square root of 16) is equivalent to the object (4). It establishes a direct and unequivocal relationship between the two entities."},
{"count_from_1_to_5": "The subject refers to the action of counting sequentially from the number one to the number five."},
{"count_from_1_to_5__is__1_2_3_4_5": "The predicate indicates the relationship of being equivalent or resulting in, specifically showing that the action of counting from 1 to 5 yields a specific set of numbers."},
{"1_2_3_4_5": "The object represents the sequence of numbers obtained by performing the action of counting from 1 to 5, which are the numbers 1, 2, 3, 4, and 5."},
{"7": "The subject, 7, is a positive integer and a prime number. It is the seventh number in the sequence of natural numbers.|Here is another description: 7 is a positive integer and a prime number. When squared, it results in the number 49, as indicated in the context."},
{"7__is__49": "The predicate \"is\" in this context signifies the relationship of equality or identity between the subject and the object. It indicates that the subject, when subjected to a certain operation, results in the object."},
{"49": "The object, 49, is the result of squaring the subject, 7. It is a perfect square and can be expressed as 7 multiplied by itself.|Here is another description: 49 is a positive integer that results from multiplying a number by itself. In this context, it is specifically the result of squaring the number 7."},
{"49__is_the_square_of__7": "\"Is the square of\" indicates a mathematical relationship where one number is the result of another number being multiplied by itself. In this case, it shows that 49 is derived from 7 through this operation."},
{"ssr_framework__mitigates__catastrophic_forgetting": "The predicate \"mitigates\" in this context refers to the SSR framework's ability to reduce or lessen the impact of catastrophic forgetting, a common problem in machine learning where new learning erases previously acquired knowledge."},
{"base_llm_\u03b8_0": "The base LLM _\u03b8_ [(0)] is the foundational language model used in the SSR framework, which is equipped with in-context learning capabilities.|Here is another description: The base LLM \u03b8 [(0)] refers to the initial version of the large language model before any fine-tuning or updates, serving as the starting point for subsequent learning processes and evaluations."},
{"base_llm_\u03b8_0__generates__synthetic_instances_\u02c6_x_\u02c6_y": "The predicate \"generates\" indicates the action performed by the base LLM _\u03b8_ [(0)], where it creates or produces something based on its learned parameters and the input it receives."},
{"synthetic_instances_\u02c6_x_\u02c6_y": "The synthetic instances _{_ (\u02c6 _x,_ \u02c6 _y_ ) _}_ are the outputs generated by the base LLM _\u03b8_ [(0)], which are pairs of input and output data used in the SSR framework to aid in mitigating catastrophic forgetting."},
{"latest_llm_\u03b8_t_1": "The latest LLM _\u03b8_ [(] _[t][\u2212]_ [1)] refers to the most recent version of a language model within a sequence of updates or iterations, specifically designed to improve upon previous versions in generating text outputs."},
{"latest_llm_\u03b8_t_1__generates__refined_output_y": "The predicate \"generates\" indicates the action performed by the latest LLM, where it produces or creates something\u2014in this case, a refined output based on input data."},
{"refined_output_y": "The refined output \u00af _y_ is the enhanced or improved text generated by the latest LLM, which is presumably more accurate or contextually appropriate than previous outputs, based on the input it received."},
{"synthetic_instances__are_selected_for__rehearsal_in_future_stages": "The phrase \"are selected for\" indicates a process where certain items, in this case, synthetic instances, are chosen based on specific criteria. This selection is crucial for ensuring that only the most relevant and high-quality instances are used in subsequent stages of the framework."},
{"rehearsal_in_future_stages": "Rehearsal in future stages refers to the practice of using selected data, such as synthetic instances, in later phases of training or refining a model. This helps in maintaining the model's performance over time, especially when dealing with issues like catastrophic forgetting."},
{"the_first_stage": "The first stage refers to the initial phase in a process where adjustments or improvements are made to a system or model. In this context, it specifically denotes the beginning of a two-stage approach aimed at enhancing the performance of a language model."},
{"the_first_stage__fine_tune__the_base_llm_\u03b8_0_on_d_1_d_1": "Fine-tune, in this context, means to make precise adjustments to the parameters of a pre-existing language model to improve its performance on a specific dataset. This process involves optimizing the model's weights to better fit the new data while retaining its general capabilities."},
{"the_base_llm_\u03b8_0_on_d_1_d_1": "The base LLM _\u03b8_ [(0)] on _D_ [(1)] = _d_ [(1)] refers to a foundational language model with initial parameters _\u03b8_ [(0)], which is being adjusted or optimized using a specific dataset _D_ [(1)], represented as _d_ [(1)]. This dataset is used to fine-tune the model, aiming to enhance its performance for the tasks it is intended to accomplish."},
{"the_catastrophic_forgetting_problem_of_llm": "The catastrophic forgetting problem of LLM refers to the issue where a language model forgets previously learned information when it is trained on new data. This problem can significantly hinder the model's performance and reliability over time."},
{"the_catastrophic_forgetting_problem_of_llm__can_be_effectively_alleviated__by_fine_tuning_the_base_llm_\u03b8_0_on_d_1_d_1": "The phrase \"can be effectively alleviated\" indicates that there is a method or process that can significantly reduce or mitigate the severity of the catastrophic forgetting problem in LLMs. This suggests that the issue is not insurmountable and can be managed with the right approach."},
{"by_fine_tuning_the_base_llm_\u03b8_0_on_d_1_d_1": "Fine-tuning the base LLM _\u03b8_ [(0)] on _D_ [(1)] = _d_ [(1)] involves adjusting the parameters of the pre-trained language model using a specific dataset. This process aims to adapt the model to new information while preserving its ability to recall previously learned data, thereby addressing the catastrophic forgetting issue."},
{"the_alleviation_of_catastrophic_forgetting": "The alleviation of catastrophic forgetting refers to the reduction or mitigation of the problem where a machine learning model, particularly a large language model (LLM), forgets previously learned information when learning new tasks or data."},
{"the_alleviation_of_catastrophic_forgetting__has_been_verified__in_previous_studies_scialom_et_al_2022_mok_et_al_2023_zhang_et_al_2023b": "The phrase \"has been verified\" indicates that the effectiveness of the method in reducing catastrophic forgetting has been confirmed and validated through empirical evidence and experimentation."},
{"in_previous_studies_scialom_et_al_2022_mok_et_al_2023_zhang_et_al_2023b": "The object \"in previous studies (Scialom et al., 2022; Mok et al., 2023; Zhang et al., 2023b)\" refers to the specific research papers where the method of alleviating catastrophic forgetting in LLMs has been tested and verified. These studies provide the empirical evidence supporting the claim."},
{"ssr_framework__involves__three_main_steps": "The predicate 'involves' indicates that the SSR framework is comprised of or includes a series of steps that are essential to its operation and effectiveness."},
{"three_main_steps": "The three main steps in the SSR framework are: 1) in-context learning based instance synthesis, which generates new instances from the context; 2) synthetic output refinement, which improves the quality of the generated instances; and 3) rehearsal with selected synthetic instances, where the model practices using the refined synthetic instances to enhance its performance."},
{"step_1": "Step 1 refers to the initial phase in the SSR framework where the process begins. It sets the stage for the subsequent steps by generating necessary instances."},
{"step_1__is__in_context_learning_based_instance_synthesis": "\"is\" serves as the linking verb that connects Step 1 to its specific function within the SSR framework, indicating what this step entails."},
{"in_context_learning_based_instance_synthesis": "In-context learning based instance synthesis is a method used in Step 1 of the SSR framework. It involves creating new instances by leveraging the context in which the learning occurs, enhancing the framework's adaptability and effectiveness."},
{"step_2": "Step 2 in the SSR framework refers to the second phase of the process, following the initial in-context learning based instance synthesis and preceding the rehearsal with selected synthetic instances."},
{"step_2__is__synthetic_output_refinement": "The predicate \"is\" in this context signifies the identification or classification of Step 2 within the SSR framework, highlighting its role and purpose in the overall process."},
{"synthetic_output_refinement": "Synthetic output refinement is the process by which the outputs generated in the first step of the SSR framework are further processed and improved to enhance their quality and suitability for the subsequent rehearsal phase."},
{"step_3": "Step 3 is the final stage in the SSR framework, following instance synthesis and output refinement. It is crucial for ensuring the framework's effectiveness by incorporating the refined synthetic instances into the rehearsal process."},
{"step_3__is__rehearsal_with_selected_synthetic_instances": "The predicate \"is\" in this context signifies the identification or classification of Step 3 within the SSR framework. It indicates that Step 3 is specifically designated as the rehearsal phase using the synthetic instances generated and refined in the previous steps."},
{"rehearsal_with_selected_synthetic_instances": "Rehearsal with selected synthetic instances refers to the process where the SSR framework utilizes the synthetic data, which has been synthesized and refined, to rehearse or practice. This step is essential for enhancing the model's performance by allowing it to learn from these carefully selected and processed instances."},
{"framework__is_illustrated_in__figure_2": "The phrase \"is illustrated in\" indicates that the framework's structure and components are visually represented or explained through a specific figure within the document."},
{"figure_2": "Figure 2 is a visual representation included in the document that provides a detailed illustration of the Self Synthesized Rehearsal (SSR) framework, showing its three main steps and how they interrelate."},
{"rehearsal_based_methods__utilize__training_instances": "The predicate \"utilize\" in this context refers to the action of employing or making use of training instances to cache and preserve the knowledge that a language model has gained from earlier stages of training."},
{"training_instances": "Training instances are specific examples or data points used during the training process of a machine learning model. In the context of rehearsal-based methods, these instances are crucial for maintaining the model's previously acquired knowledge, especially when the original training data is limited or unavailable.|Here is another description: Training instances refer to the specific examples or data points used in the training process of a machine learning model. These instances are crucial as they provide the necessary information for the model to learn and generalize from."},
{"training_instances__cache__knowledge_acquired_by_the_llm": "The predicate \"cache\" in this context signifies the process of storing or retaining information. Specifically, it refers to the method by which rehearsal-based approaches store the knowledge that the Language Model (LLM) has gained from prior training stages to be utilized in subsequent stages."},
{"knowledge_acquired_by_the_llm": "The object \"knowledge acquired by the LLM\" denotes the information and patterns that the Language Model has learned and internalized during its training process. This knowledge is essential for the model to perform tasks and make predictions accurately."},
{"real_world_scenarios": "Real-world scenarios refer to practical situations and applications where technologies are implemented and tested in actual environments rather than theoretical or simulated settings. In the context of machine learning and large language models (LLMs), real-world scenarios involve deploying models in everyday tasks and problem-solving activities."},
{"real_world_scenarios__use__publicly_released_llm_checkpoint": "The predicate \"use\" in this context signifies the application or employment of a specific resource or tool\u2014in this case, a publicly-released LLM checkpoint\u2014to achieve a particular goal or perform a task. It implies the utilization of the checkpoint as a foundational element in the deployment or operation of the LLM within practical settings."},
{"publicly_released_llm_checkpoint": "A publicly-released LLM checkpoint is a saved state of a large language model that has been made available to the public. This checkpoint includes the model's parameters and configurations at a specific point in its training process, allowing others to load and use the model without needing to train it from scratch. It serves as a starting point for further fine-tuning or deployment in various applications."},
{"original_training_data__may_be__limited": "The phrase \"may be\" indicates a possibility or a condition that suggests uncertainty or limitation. In this context, it implies that there is a potential constraint or scarcity regarding the availability of the original training data."},
{"limited": "The term \"limited\" describes a situation where the quantity, scope, or availability of something is restricted. In the context of the original training data, it suggests that there might not be enough data available for effective use, especially in real-world scenarios where a pre-released LLM checkpoint is employed."},
{"to_address_this_limitation": "The subject refers to the approach taken to tackle the limitation of limited availability of original training data when using a publicly-released LLM checkpoint in real-world scenarios."},
{"to_address_this_limitation__generate__rehearsal_training_instances_synthetically": "The predicate involves the process of creating or producing something, in this case, rehearsal training instances, to compensate for the lack of original training data."},
{"rehearsal_training_instances_synthetically": "The object describes the outcome of the predicate, which is the synthetic generation of rehearsal training instances. These instances are created to retain previously acquired knowledge by the LLM and are generated using the in-context learning capability of LLMs."},
{"llm__follow__abstract_instructions": "The predicate, 'follow', describes the action or capability of the LLM to adhere to or execute given instructions, specifically in the context of generating synthetic training instances based on demonstrations."},
{"abstract_instructions": "The object, 'abstract instructions', refers to high-level directives or guidelines that are not concrete or specific, which the LLM is tasked to interpret and act upon to generate new data instances."},
{"in_context_learning_capability": "In-context learning capability refers to the ability of a language model to adapt and learn from examples provided within the context of a specific task or instruction, without requiring explicit retraining."},
{"in_context_learning_capability__leverage__for_instance_synthesis": "Leverage in this context means utilizing or taking advantage of the in-context learning capability to achieve a specific goal, such as generating synthetic training instances."},
{"for_instance_synthesis": "For instance synthesis refers to the process of creating new, synthetic examples or instances that can be used for training or rehearsal purposes, based on the in-context learning capability of the language model."},
{"training_stage_t": "The training stage _t_ represents a specific phase in the iterative process of training a language model, where the model is being updated and refined based on new data and instructions."},
{"training_stage_t__acquire__k_demonstrations_xk_yk_k_k_1": "The predicate \"acquire\" in this context refers to the action of obtaining or gathering a set of demonstrations during a particular training stage to aid in the learning process of the language model."},
{"k_demonstrations_xk_yk_k_k_1": "The object \"_K_ demonstrations _{_ ( _xk, yk_ ) _}_ _[K]_ _k_ =1\" denotes a collection of _K_ pairs of input-output examples (_xk, yk_) that are used as demonstrations to guide the language model's learning during the training stage _t_."},
{"demonstrations__can_be_collected_from__previous_instruction_data_d_t_1": "The phrase \"can be collected from\" indicates a source or method through which demonstrations are obtained. In this scenario, it suggests that demonstrations are derived or gathered from a specific dataset or previous instruction data, ensuring continuity and retention of learned knowledge across different training stages."},
{"previous_instruction_data_d_t_1": "Previous instruction data _d_ [(] _[t][\u2212]_ [1)] refers to the dataset or collection of instructions used in the immediately preceding training stage (t-1). This data serves as a reservoir from which new demonstrations can be drawn to maintain and build upon the knowledge acquired by the language model in earlier stages."},
{"demonstrations__can_be_manually_constructed_containing__similar_knowledge_to_d_t_1": "The phrase \"can be manually constructed containing\" describes the process of creating demonstrations by hand, ensuring that they embody specific knowledge or characteristics. This manual construction is crucial when original training data is not available, allowing the model to still learn from curated examples that reflect the necessary information."},
{"similar_knowledge_to_d_t_1": "\"Similar knowledge to _d_ [(] _[t][\u2212]_ [1)]\" refers to the knowledge contained in the demonstrations from the previous training stage (_t-1_). These demonstrations serve as a reference point for creating new demonstrations, ensuring continuity and coherence in the knowledge the model acquires across different training stages."},
{"all_demonstrations": "All demonstrations refer to a collection of example pairs (_xk, yk_) gathered either from previous instruction data or manually constructed to retain knowledge across training stages in rehearsal-based methods."},
{"all_demonstrations__concatenate_and_utilize__the_base_llm": "Concatenate and utilize describes the process of merging all collected demonstrations into a single input and then employing the base LLM to generate synthetic training instances, ensuring the model retains and applies previously acquired knowledge."},
{"the_base_llm": "The base LLM is the foundational language model used in the process. It takes the concatenated demonstrations as input to produce new synthetic instances, aiding in the model's learning and adaptation during training stages.|Here is another description: The base LLM, or base Large Language Model, is the foundational model from which other models or refinements are derived. In this scenario, it is the source of data noise, implying that inaccuracies or errors in the data are a result of limitations or issues within the base LLM itself."},
{"synthetic_instance_\u02c6_x_\u02c6_y": "The synthetic instance (\u02c6 _x,_ \u02c6 _y_ ) represents a pair of input and output generated synthetically to serve as training data. This instance is created to mimic real training examples, allowing the LLM to rehearse and retain previously acquired knowledge even when original training data is not available."},
{"synthetic_instance_\u02c6_x_\u02c6_y__generate_using__llm_concat_k_k_1_x_k_y_k_\u03b8_0": "The predicate \"generate using\" describes the process by which the synthetic instance is created. It involves leveraging the in-context learning capability of the LLM, where the model follows abstract instructions to produce new data instances based on provided demonstrations."},
{"llm_concat_k_k_1_x_k_y_k_\u03b8_0": "The object LLM(concat _[K]_ _k_ =1 [(] _[x][k][, y][k]_ [);] _[ \u03b8]_ [(0)]) represents the Language Model (LLM) used in the generation process. This LLM concatenates a set of _K_ demonstrations and uses its initial parameters (\u03b8(0)) to generate the synthetic instance. The demonstrations consist of pairs (x_k, y_k) that guide the LLM in creating new, synthetic training data."},
{"d": "D is a mathematical notation representing a specific function or variable in the context of an equation.|Here is another description: The object, D, represents a larger mathematical expression, system, or set that encompasses the subject, d(t). It is the broader context in which d(t) exists or operates."},
{"d__is_defined_as__d_t_t_1d_i": "\"is defined as\" indicates that the subject D is being explicitly described or characterized by the expression that follows."},
{"d_t_t_1d_i": "d(t)[\ufffd] - t[\u2212]1d(i) is a mathematical expression involving the function d evaluated at t, modified by some operation indicated by [\ufffd], and then subtracted by the product of t raised to the power of -1 and d evaluated at i."},
{"d_t__is_part_of__d": "The predicate, \"is part of,\" indicates that the subject, d(t), is a constituent element or subset within the larger entity or system denoted by the object, D."},
