{"good_alignment_with_human_evaluation": "Good alignment with human evaluation refers to the metric's capability to produce results that closely match the judgments made by humans when assessing the quality of generated text. This alignment is crucial for ensuring that the metric effectively captures the nuances and subtleties of human language understanding."},
{"wang_et_al_2022__demonstrate__good_alignment_of_rouge_l_with_human_evaluation": "The predicate \"demonstrate\" indicates that the study by Wang et al. (2022) provides evidence or proof of a particular claim or observation. In this context, it shows that the study offers empirical support for the alignment of a specific metric with human judgment."},
{"good_alignment_of_rouge_l_with_human_evaluation": "\"Good alignment of ROUGE-L with human evaluation\" refers to the finding that the ROUGE-L metric, which is used to assess the quality of text generated by language models, correlates well with the judgments made by human evaluators. This suggests that ROUGE-L is an effective measure for evaluating the performance of language models in tasks that require natural language generation."},
{"lopez_paz_and_ranzato_2017": "Lopez-Paz and Ranzato (2017) refers to a study or work conducted by the authors Lopez-Paz and Ranzato in the year 2017. This work is relevant in the context of evaluating performance metrics for language models, particularly in tasks involving open-ended sequence generation."},
{"lopez_paz_and_ranzato_2017__consider__metrics_based_on_rouge_l": "The predicate \"consider\" in this context means that Lopez-Paz and Ranzato (2017) took into account or evaluated certain metrics. Specifically, they looked at metrics that are based on the ROUGE-L metric, which is used to assess the performance of language models."},
{"metrics_based_on_rouge_l": "Metrics based on ROUGE-L are evaluation metrics derived from the ROUGE-L metric, which measures the overlap of n-grams between a generated text and a reference text. In the context provided, these metrics are used to evaluate the performance of a language model (LLM) on various tasks, following the approach suggested by Lopez-Paz and Ranzato (2017)."},
