{"this_fine_tuned_llm": "This fine-tuned Large Language Model (LLM) refers to a specific version of an LLM that has been adjusted and optimized for particular tasks or datasets. It represents an advanced stage of model development where the general capabilities of the LLM have been enhanced to perform better in targeted applications."},
{"this_fine_tuned_llm__has__limitation_in_terms_of_icl_capability": "The predicate \"has\" in this context signifies the possession or characteristic of the subject. It indicates that the fine-tuned LLM is associated with a specific attribute or limitation, which is crucial for understanding its performance and capabilities."},
{"limitation_in_terms_of_icl_capability": "The limitation in terms of In-Context Learning (ICL) capability refers to the constraints or boundaries within which the fine-tuned LLM operates regarding its ability to learn and adapt from context provided within the input. This suggests that while the model is fine-tuned, it may not fully leverage the potential of ICL to the same extent as other models or under different conditions."},
{"icl_with_input_only_demonstrations": "ICL with input-only demonstrations refers to a method where a language model is fine-tuned using only input examples without corresponding output annotations. This approach is tested to evaluate the model's capability in few-shot learning scenarios."},
{"icl_with_input_only_demonstrations__yields__comparable_performance": "The term \"yields\" in this context means that the method of using ICL with input-only demonstrations results in or produces a certain outcome. Specifically, it indicates the effectiveness or performance of the method when applied."},
{"comparable_performance": "Comparable performance refers to the result achieved by the ICL method with input-only demonstrations, which is on par with or similar to the performance achieved by other methods that might use more comprehensive data, such as those including output annotations. This suggests that the method is effective and robust."},
{"output_annotations": "Output annotations refer to the additional information or labels provided alongside the outputs in a learning process. In the context of fine-tuned language models, these annotations might include details about the correctness or quality of the generated outputs."},
{"output_annotations__are__not_essential_for_icl": "The predicate \"are\" signifies a state of being or existence. In this context, it indicates the status or necessity of output annotations in the process of In-Context Learning (ICL)."},
{"not_essential_for_icl": "The statement \"not essential for ICL\" suggests that output annotations are not a critical component for the effectiveness of In-Context Learning. This implies that ICL can achieve comparable performance even without these annotations, highlighting the robustness of the learning method."},
{"the_robustness_of_ssr": "The robustness of SSR refers to the ability of the system to maintain consistent performance under various conditions, particularly in the context of fine-tuned language models and input-context learning (ICL) scenarios."},
{"the_robustness_of_ssr__is__verified": "The predicate \"is\" in this context signifies a state of being or condition, specifically indicating that the robustness of SSR has been confirmed or proven through the given experiments and observations."},
{"verified": "The verification of the robustness of SSR indicates that the system has been tested and shown to perform reliably, even when certain elements like output annotations are removed, thus confirming its stability and effectiveness in different configurations."},
